  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Database Seeder**\n\nI\'m currently focused on designing a node script, specifically `seed_db.cjs`, to populate the SQLite database `sisti.db` with realistic random data. The main goal is to ensure a smooth operation that doesn\'t inadvertently remove the admin user (`admin@hpsvp.org.br`) during the seeding process.\n\n\n**Checking Existing Users**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to preserving existing user data. Now, my script will read the `users` table in `sisti.db` to identify and retain any existing users, preventing loss of login credentials. Instead of deleting all rows, the plan is to selectively clear other tables and then repopulate them, adding more random users while keeping existing ones intact.\n\n\n**Adding Random Users**\n\nI\'m now generating 0-15 random users with various roles, like \'Administrador\', \'T\303\251cnico\', and \'Usu\303\241rio\', ensuring their passwords are encrypted using `bcryptjs`. This process works smoothly since the library is included as a dependency. I\'ve confirmed `bcryptjs` is listed in the dependencies. Now, I\'m verifying the database structures defined in `server.ts` to ensure compatibility with my seeding process, looking at tables like `users`, `branches`, and `equipments`.\n\n\n**Defining Database Structures**\n\nI\'m now detailing the database structure for seeding, focusing on tables such as `subnets`, `tickets`, `incidents`, and `system_settings`. I\'ve added these tables and their respective fields to my seeding process. I have confirmed that the `sisti.db` file will be the target database, and I am developing the node script `seed_db.cjs` to populate all these tables with realistic Brazilian Portuguese data. I\'m focusing on ensuring data consistency and relevance to the application\'s context. I will write the code to check for pre-existing users and preserve them. I plan on reading the existing `users`, clearing the database safely, and re-inserting those users.\n\n\n**Preserving Existing Data**\n\nI\'m now implementing the preservation of existing user accounts. The plan is to read all existing rows from the `users` table into memory. Then, I will clear the `users` table entirely. I\'ll re-insert the saved existing users, along with adding new random users. If there are no users, the seed will create default admin and tech accounts, ensuring login functionality.\n\n\n**Populating Database Tables**\n\nI\'m now focusing on seeding the remaining tables. I will use transactions and `DELETE FROM table` to clear them, then populate them in the correct order, starting with `branches` and `departments`. I will reinsert saved users and generate random users. For user creation, I am using pre-defined email formats and hashing passwords with `bcryptjs` using a constant password, if it is not found to already have been hashed, focusing on realistic data with Brazilian Portuguese names and roles.\n\n\n**Generating Realistic Subnets**\n\nI\'m now generating realistic subnets, focusing on the `subnets` table. I\'m taking the network definitions into account, populating fields such as network name, base IP, description, start and end IP suffixes, gateway suffix, broadcast suffix, and CIDR. The goal is to create subnets that align with typical network configurations, ensuring they are related to pre-defined network segments.\n\n\n**Populating Additional Tables**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating data for `equipments`, `ip_addresses`, and `tickets` to enrich the database. I\'m focusing on populating the `equipments` table with 20 random devices. These include \"Computador\", \"Impressora\", and others, with realistic brands and details like serial numbers and warranty dates, tied to their respective branch and department. Then I\'m generating IP addresses tied to networks and equipments, and finally, seeding 40-50 tickets with varied statuses, priorities, descriptions, and assignees.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view `src/types.ts` to understand the domain models and ensure all seeded fields match the types used in the frontend."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 14
      nanos: 394608252
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
