tion. The code\'s elegant, robust, and completely removes errors. I can now start creating the seeder script. First, I\'ll list the mock data.\n\n\n**Listing Mock Data**\n\nI\'ve moved on to listing the mock data, now specifying `branches`, `departments`, and `users`. I have listed several branches with IDs, names, and cities. Then, I have detailed several departments, also detailing their names, branchId, and heads. Now, I have to list users.\n\n\n**Generating User Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user data generation within `seed_db.cjs`. I\'ll first retrieve existing users, then re-insert them to preserve their credentials. If I need more users, I\'ll generate new ones. I\'m aiming for at least 15 users in total, to simulate an active system. I\'ve got a sample list of users.\n\n\n**Creating User Data Objects**\n\nI\'m now transforming the listed user details into usable data objects for insertion into the database. I\'m focusing on ensuring each object aligns perfectly with the expected column structure, including name, email, role, and department/branch assignments. I am now preparing the SQL insert statements.\n\n\n**Implementing Password Hashing**\n\nI\'m now implementing password hashing and email uniqueness checks within user data generation. I\'ll hash passwords using `bcrypt` for security, ensuring no plain-text passwords. To avoid duplicate emails, I\'ll leverage a `Set` for efficient email lookups, skipping existing ones. I\'m focusing on only inserting new users.\n\n\n\n**Defining Ticket Data**\n\nI\'m now incorporating ticket data into the seed file. I\'ve prepared a sample set of tickets with various priorities and SLAs, including incidents like Wi-Fi instability and network configuration requests. Now, I\'m defining network data.\n\nI have also defined networks, with names, base IPs, and descriptions. These include administrative, medical equipment, Wi-Fi, and VoIP networks.\n\n\n**Defining Network and Equipment Data**\n\nI\'m now integrating network and equipment details into the seeding process, specifically subnets and equipment. I\'ve defined several subnets with details like IDs, names, CIDR, and IP ranges. Now, I\'m generating a randomized list of 25 equipments. I have also prepared the `manufacturers` array. I\'ll need to define the relationship between equipment and network data.\n\n\n**Generating Equipment Details**\n\nI\'m now generating 25 equipment entries with UUIDs, realistic names, serial numbers, asset numbers, and manufacturer data, alongside random branch and department assignments. I\'m also creating 20 assigned IP addresses, linking them to specific equipment and networks, assigning hostnames as well.\n\n\n**Crafting Ticket Details**\n\nI\'m now generating 40 realistic tickets spanning the last 30 days, incorporating data on the MAC addresses of equipments. I\'ll distribute these tickets across different statuses and priorities to simulate real-world scenarios. I\'m also ensuring they are linked to realistic users, technicians, and potentially equipment, enhancing data realism. I\'ll ensure ticket generation respects valid incident relationships.\n\n\n"
