  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Length**\n\nOkay, I\'ve hit a bit of a wall. The data is getting truncated because `/src/pages/Dashboard.tsx` is a beast, clocking in at 1633 lines. I\'ve managed to peek at the initial 1600 lines, which thankfully includes the \"Incidents\" charts. Time to dig deeper into specific lines!\n\n\n**Pinpointing Chart Origin**\n\nI\'ve located the start of the \"Top 10 Incidents\" pie chart block in the code. It begins on line 562 within `Dashboard.tsx`. This section, marked by a comment, contains a `motion.div` component. It\'s using `variants`, `whileHover`, and an `onClick` event, suggesting it\'s interactive and animates when hovered. The styling appears to be handling both light and dark modes.\n\n\n**Examining Incident Visualization**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the code that generates the pie chart for \"Top 10 Incidents.\" Lines 572-578 define the container, and 580-595 configure the chart with `ResponsiveContainer` and `PieChart` components. I am confirming the chart\'s data is rendered conditionally, and that the `Pie` component is being passed key attributes.\n\n\n**Tracing Data Source**\n\nI\'ve traced the `topIncidentsData` back to its origin. It\'s derived from `recentTickets`, specifically lines 336-341 in `Dashboard.tsx`. A `reduce` function is used to aggregate incident counts, generating the data for the pie chart. The user\'s feedback about the chart\'s layout and the need for a fixed center, combined with abbreviated incident names (\"TIPO\") is also noted, and will be incorporated into the next steps of my investigation.\n\n\n**Investigating Chart Centering**\n\nI\'m now investigating why the \"Incidents\" pie chart isn\'t centered, contrasting it with the centered \"SLA Status\" chart. I have observed the \"SLA Status\" chart has `cx=\"50%\"` and `cy=\"50%\"` defined within the `Pie` component, and it lacks a `<Legend />` in its core chart. Instead, the legend is manually drawn below the chart, which I suspect contributes to the uniform layout and centering. The \"Incidents\" chart, however, doesn\'t appear to handle its legend in this manner.\n\n\n**Addressing Chart Misalignment**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the root cause of the \"Incidents\" pie chart misalignment. The Recharts `<Legend />` component, located at lines 615-622 of `Dashboard.tsx`, is the culprit. Its dynamic size, driven by lengthy incident names like \"Acesso Indispon\303\255vel ao Sistema PEP Hospitalar\", pushes the chart and pie upwards, disrupting centering. To resolve this, I\'m considering abbreviating the incident names, only using the \"TIPO\" identifier, and exploring alternative legend placements or styling. My attention will shift to the `server.ts` file next to see how `incidentName` is defined.\n\n\n**Examining Ticket Properties**\n\nI\'m now investigating the properties of the `ticket` object, specifically the `incidentName`. I need to understand the relationship between \"TIPO\" and the full incident title. Lines 337-341 in `Dashboard.tsx` are where the `incidentCounts` are generated, using `ticket.incidentName`. I will be inspecting the `Dashboard.tsx` and the `server.ts` files, and also the `seed_db.cjs` file, to clarify how the `ticket` object is populated and how the full incident names are stored. I have found the `incident` name property in the `seed_db.cjs` file!\n\n\n**Confirming Incident Names**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the incident names in `seed_db.cjs`. I can see the full incident titles are stored there, such as \"Acesso Indispon\303\255vel ao Sistema PEP Hospitalar\". I\'m now looking at the structure. The titles have descriptive names, which is a good thing for reporting, but I believe I can make the chart readable if only the TIPO value is displayed. I\'ve also noted that the titles are consistently formatted.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "Irei analisar o arquivo principal de backend `server.ts` para entender como o servi\303\247o de API retorna as informa\303\247\303\265es de chamados (tickets) e incidentes, permitindo-nos verificar de onde vem e como \303\251 estruturado o nome do incidente versus o t\303\255tulo/chamado aberto."
