ntered and others aren\'t, looking closely at how legends are handled, particularly in the SLA graph. My goal is to grasp the centering logic and how it might apply to the incident graph. I\'m also considering how to extract abbreviated incident names from the existing data, perhaps by capturing parenthetical content or simplifying the main name.\n\n\n**Comparing Graph Structures**\n\nI\'m now comparing the SLA graph and the \"Incidentes por Equipamento\" graph within `Dashboard.tsx`. The key difference I see is the inclusion of a `<Legend />` component and its position in the second graph. I\'m trying to figure out if this is related to the centering issue I observed. The lack of legend in the SLA graph seems to be an important factor.\n\n\n**Investigating Centering Mechanism**\n\nI\'m now examining the `PieChart` component, specifically focusing on how the `cx` and `cy` attributes of the `Pie` are defined within `Dashboard.tsx`, lines 580-588, and how these parameters affect the chart centering and possible resizing. I\'m focusing on why Recharts is trying to resize and reposition the chart due to the legend. I\'m trying to determine if legend items are causing the issues.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Root Cause**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on why the pie chart might be misbehaving with long names. It seems excessive text, like ticket titles, can cause line breaks and shift the chart off-center. I\'m now clarifying the user\'s intent. Do they want an abbreviated incident name, or the ticket title? I\'m recalling the seeded database, which stores `incidentName` effectively. I\'m exploring if the original, un-seeded database might have had null `incidentName` fields, and the system used the ticket title as a fallback. Or maybe there were strange seeding data? I am now looking at the code, in the backend, that defines `ticket.incidentName`.\n\n\n"
